wasi itself has no subprocesses or TCP, so the child-process pool was
stubbed to fail with Unsupported and pre-16.2 turbopack could not run
postcss/tailwind/webpack loaders at all. But the napi host usually CAN do
both: plain Node has child_process/net, and node-compatible wasi runtimes
(strapkit) provide them as guest syscalls. Same move as the host fetch
bridge: the binding exposes initTurbopackProcessBridge(spawn, write,
action, listen) and the pool's Command/Child/TcpListener/TcpStream become
host-backed types (src/host_pool.rs, replacing wasi_stubs.rs). The pool's
length-prefixed protocol runs unchanged over the bridged streams, and pool
children are stock node processes running turbopack's own ipc entrypoint —
they never load the binding. No bridge registered keeps the old
Unsupported behavior. Native builds untouched (wasm-only module + deps).
